Thanks all, 

I have updated the proposal in the top. pasting here the original proposal just for the record

```C#
struct CancellationToken
{
    public CancellationTokenRegistration Register(Action<CancellationToken, object> callback, object state);
    public CancellationTokenRegistration Register(Action<CancellationToken, object> callback, object state, bool useSynchronizationContext);
    public CancellationTokenRegistration UnsafeRegister(Action<CancellationToken, object> callback, object state);
}
```

[Video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5nMDURGNsQ0&t=0h13m51s)

* Looks good as proposed
* We omitted the `bool useSynchronizationContext` as we don't want to encourage its use. If needed, we can add it later. The behavior of these new methods is as if it was `false`.

```C#
namespace System.Threading
{
    public struct CancellationToken
    {
        public CancellationTokenRegistration Register(Action<object?, CancellationToken> callback, object? state);
        public CancellationTokenRegistration UnsafeRegister(Action<object?, CancellationToken> callback, object? state);
    }
}
```

Why is the method signature forcing `object?` type for the state? Wouldn't be better to have something like this instead?

```c#
namespace System.Threading
{
    public struct CancellationToken
    {
        public CancellationTokenRegistration Register<T>(Action<T, CancellationToken> callback, T state);
        public CancellationTokenRegistration UnsafeRegister<T>(Action<T, CancellationToken> callback, T state);
    }
}
```

> I'm concern about usability for seasonal developers

`object state` shows up in tons of APIs. Why this one in particular?  This overload is already for cases where developers are thinking through higher performance; otherwise, they'd just capture the state they need via closure:
```C#
... = cancellationToken.Register(() =>
{
    ... // do whatever using whatever state you want... simplest API for "seasonal developers"
});
```

> The implementation would stay the same

I'm not sure what you mean.  How would the delegate be invoked?
